WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.720
INTRO:
Nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:05.360
likes bailouts. The idea that rotting&nbsp;
companies that exploit their customers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.840
their employees, the environment, and who knows&nbsp;
what else are able to receive a bailout from tax&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:16.080
payer money just because the company is quote&nbsp;
on quote “too big to fail” is disgusting. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.040
official reasoning for bailouts is that if these&nbsp;
companies were to collapse, hundreds of thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:25.840
of jobs would be lost and the subsequent&nbsp;
economic shockwaves would be catastrophic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.280
But, maybe that’s actually better than keeping&nbsp;
these parasitic corporations alive, but that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:36.640
besides the point. The point is that bailouts&nbsp;
have a terrible reputation and for good reason.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.400
However, there have been a few bailouts in history&nbsp;
that have actually played out surprisingly well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.560
You would think that this would be most likely&nbsp;
to happen with a small company that’s nimble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:51.200
and quick. But, this actually happened&nbsp;
with the biggest bailout in history:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:56.320
AIG. AIG was an insurance/finanical&nbsp;
conglomerate that had suffered insane losses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:03.040
so the government came to their rescue with an&nbsp;
appalling $182 billion. The bailout itself is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.760
large enough to be world’s 60th largest company.&nbsp;
Now, I don’t support this bailout, but the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:15.600
government did end up profiting from the bailout&nbsp;
and things worked out surprisingly well. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:25.840
here’s why a company even needed a $182 billion&nbsp;
bailout and why the bailout actually kinda worked.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.800
BUILT ON HARDSHIP:
$182 billion is an insane amount of money, so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.720
how did a company even end up in such a terrible&nbsp;
position? Well, the answer is half a century worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:43.120
of terrible decisions that eventually caught up.&nbsp;
AIG wasn’t always bad though. Like most companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:47.600
AIG was started with good intentions&nbsp;
by a man named Cornelius Vander Starr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:53.440
Cornelius was born to a Dutch family living in&nbsp;
Fort Bragg California in 1892. Things started off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.720
good for Cornelius when he was a baby. His father&nbsp;
had a respectable job as a railroad engineer and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:04.000
the family was able to comfortably get by. But,&nbsp;
this didn’t last long as his father would pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.920
away when Cornelius was just two years old. This&nbsp;
forced the family to take in boarders to get by.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:14.320
If you don’t know what this is, it’s basically&nbsp;
converting your house into a long term airbnb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.720
to earn money. Considering this background, you&nbsp;
would think that Cornelius would play things safe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.160
when it came to his career, but he was more of&nbsp;
a risk taker. He would drop out of UC Berkeley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.360
after just one year and would try opening an&nbsp;
ice cream shop. This was somewhat successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:35.600
but nothing crazy, so he would end up joining the&nbsp;
US army near the end of WW1. Cornelius never got&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.960
deployed but he could feel this urge to travel&nbsp;
and see the world, so he would get a job as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:47.120
clerk at a Japanese office and move to Japan. From&nbsp;
there, he would travel to Shanghai, China 1919,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.040
and this where he would found AIG, though it&nbsp;
wasn’t called AIG at the time. The young company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.840
focused on providing western insurance products to&nbsp;
the Asian market which proved to be quite popular.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.480
Soon enough, Cornelius had operations across all&nbsp;
of Southeast Asia along with the Philippines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.920
Indonesia, and Malaysia. It wasn’t until he had a&nbsp;
strong presence in Asia that Cornelius even opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.440
an office in America. But, even after opening an&nbsp;
American office, Cornelius didn’t really pursue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:18.960
the American market. Maybe, he thought it was&nbsp;
too competitive. His reasoning is not too clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.960
but something that was clear was that AIG&nbsp;
was phenomenal at attracting customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:27.520
in developing countries. So, naturally,&nbsp;
Cornelius would turn his attention to Cuba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:33.440
and Latin America. It wasn’t until WW2 hit that&nbsp;
Cornelius finally shifted his focus to America.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:37.440
You see, Cornelius saw an opportunity to&nbsp;
provide insurance for military personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:41.840
which turned out to be extremely popular. Moving&nbsp;
past the war, Cornelius would spend his time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:47.760
expanding across devastated European countries&nbsp;
like France, Italy, Germany, and the UK. Clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:53.360
Cornelius was no saint. He built AIG on the&nbsp;
backs of other people’s hardships. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.480
he leveraged people from underdeveloped countries.&nbsp;
Then, he leveraged military families. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:04.240
he leveraged war stricken Europe. Cornelius was&nbsp;
no doubt an opportunist, but something he wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:12.960
was a fraud. When he was at the helm, AIG was&nbsp;
sound financially. But, naturally, Cornelius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.600
couldn’t run the company forever. So, he would&nbsp;
end up handing the company over to a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:23.200
Maurice R Greenberg also known as Hank in the&nbsp;
1960s, and this marked the beginning of the end.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:27.600
FUNDAMENTAL COLLAPSE:&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.200
Ever since Hank joined AIG, he only had one&nbsp;
goal which was to impress his superiors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.240
At first, this was Cornelius, and eventually,&nbsp;
this would become the shareholders. Now, trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.760
to impress your superiors isn’t a bad goal per&nbsp;
se, but the problem was that Hank was willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.600
do almost anything and this was evident from the&nbsp;
very beginning. When Hank was hired in 1960, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:55.120
was given the task of developing an international&nbsp;
accident and health business. But, his approach to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.120 --> 00:05:00.160
doing so was completely different from what we saw&nbsp;
with Cornerlius. Much of Cornelius’ success could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:05.680
be attributed to his patience. He never forced&nbsp;
an oppurtunity. He was willing to wait years if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.960
not decades for the right opportunity. It doesn’t&nbsp;
seem like Hank was a fan of this approach though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:15.760
as he far preferred to create his own opportunity.&nbsp;
And the way he accomplished this was by leveraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:20.720
insurance brokers instead of insurance agents.&nbsp;
For most of you guys, these probably sound like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.680
the same thing, but theres a small difference.&nbsp;
You see, insurance agents are employed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.560
insurance company meaning that their loyalty lies&nbsp;
with the company. It is the agent’s best interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.960
to find the best clients to insure. Insurance&nbsp;
brokers on the other hand are technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:40.480
supposed to be loyal to the customer, but really,&nbsp;
the only thing their loyal to is the commission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:45.680
It’s in the broker’s best interest to complete as&nbsp;
many transactions as possible. The quality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:50.800
transactions doesn’t really matter to them. So,&nbsp;
from the company’s perspective, in the long term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.000
it’s better to use agents than brokers because&nbsp;
while agents are more expensive, they’ll secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.200
higher quality clients and protect the liquidity&nbsp;
of the firm. But, Hank through all of that out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.040
window and went all in on brokers. He figured that&nbsp;
even if sales went down, the company would be more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.960
profitable thanks to the small commissions&nbsp;
that brokers charged. In the short term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:16.560
this made him look his a genius as his departments&nbsp;
grew faster than ever. But, what he was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:27.280
doing was gambling the financial integrity of&nbsp;
the firm for more revenue and profits. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.680
Hank was simply cutting corners here and there&nbsp;
to inflate the company’s profits, but eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:36.960
he would turn to full on accounting fraud. In the&nbsp;
early 2000s, Hank orchestrated multiple fraudulent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:41.440
deals with offshore entities that made it seem&nbsp;
like AIG’s reserves were larger than they truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.680
were. These deals also inflated AIG’s profits by&nbsp;
nearly $4 billion. When these activities came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:54.960
light in 2005, Hank would be ousted from AIG after&nbsp;
leading the company for 37 years. But the damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:06:59.600
had already been done. While Hank had succeeded&nbsp;
in growing the company’s marketcap from a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.000
hundred million dollars to a few hundred billion&nbsp;
dollars and made AIG the world’s largest insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.000 --> 00:07:08.800
company, he had also destroyed the company&nbsp;
fundamentally. All it would take is one gust&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:15.840
of wind to blow over this house of cards,&nbsp;
and that’s exactly what happened in 2008.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.920
PUBLIC COLLAPSE:&nbsp;

00:07:17.920 --> 00:07:23.600
In the end, it was two risky practices that did&nbsp;
in AIG and these were credit default swaps and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:28.080
securities lending. Credit default swaps&nbsp;
are pretty complex financial instruments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:33.440
but for our purposes, we can just think of them&nbsp;
as a type of insurance. If you have home insurance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.400
and something terrible happens to your home, the&nbsp;
insurance company would cover the damages right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:43.040
Well, it works the same way with credit default&nbsp;
swaps, but instead of insuring your home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:48.640
they insure corporate debt. For example, let’s&nbsp;
say GM needs money to build a new factory.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.480
You as a lender are willing to lend GM this money.&nbsp;
But you wanna make sure that even if GM can’t pay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:59.680
back their debts, you get your money back. So, you&nbsp;
get insurance on this debt called a credit default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:05.600
swap. This way, even if GM collapses, AIG will pay&nbsp;
you back a certain percentage or even the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.200
loan based on the terms of the swap. But, the&nbsp;
thing to note is that AIG doesn’t actually have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:16.000
enough money to pay back all of these swaps. It’s&nbsp;
unlikely that all of these corporations default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.000
at the same time. So, AIG only has to carry a&nbsp;
portion of the total amount they insure. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.280
this wouldn’t be a problem if these swaps were&nbsp;
a small portion of their portfolio. But, AIG had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:34.080
written swaps on $500 billion in assets. And as we&nbsp;
now know, corporations would fall left and right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:40.000
throughout 2008 meaning that AIG had start paying&nbsp;
out massive checks. But, this wasn’t even what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.720
screwed them over. What screwed them over was a&nbsp;
downgrade in their credit rating on September 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:49.840
2008. This downgrade meant that AIG had to put&nbsp;
up significantly more collateral to back up their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:55.200
credit default swaps, collateral they didn’t&nbsp;
have. I think you can guess what happened next.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:59.440
It wasn’t just credit default swaps that&nbsp;
were screwing over the company though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:04.560
They were also getting burned with securities&nbsp;
lending. As an insurance company, AIG owned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.520
millions of shares of stock, bonds, options,&nbsp;
futures, variable annuities and basically every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:15.360
other security you can think of. Now, AIG could’ve&nbsp;
just kept these securities for themselves. But AIG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:19.680
wasn’t satisfied with the returns that these&nbsp;
securities could offer by themselves. So, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.640
decided to lend out these securities in return&nbsp;
for interest. This is a pretty common practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:29.920
amongst financial institutions, and it wouldn’t&nbsp;
have been that bad if AIG stopped right here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:36.240
but they would take it to even the next level.&nbsp;
You see, when someone borrows a security from AIG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.240 --> 00:09:40.880
aside from paying interest on the security,&nbsp;
they also have to give AIG cash collateral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.360
The borrower can pay back the security at any&nbsp;
time and ask for their cash collateral back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.720
So, it’s in AIG’s best interest to keep the&nbsp;
cash collateral as liquid as possible. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:56.160
AIG decided that it would be a great idea to&nbsp;
invest this cash into sub prime mortgages. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.160 --> 00:10:00.720
the financial crisis hit, a lot of AIG’s borrowers&nbsp;
were trying to raise cash for the recession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:04.320
So, they paid back their securities and&nbsp;
asked AIG for their cash collateral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:10.560
But, you know this cash was invested in subprime&nbsp;
mortgages which um completely collapsed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:17.840
yeah it was bad. At this point, it was just&nbsp;
a matter of time until AIG needed a bailout.

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.200
THE BAILOUT:&nbsp;

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:26.640
AIG would go onto post some of the worst corporate&nbsp;
quarters known to mankind. On this channel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:32.480
we often discuss market cap losses, but the losses&nbsp;
that we’re about to discuss are not market cap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:40.640
losses, they’re cash losses. In Q3 of 2008, AIG&nbsp;
would report a net loss of $24.5 billion which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:47.360
was 8th worst corporate quarter ever since. And&nbsp;
that wasn’t even the worst of it. In Q4 of 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:54.160
AIG would lose $61.7 billion which is the worst&nbsp;
corporate quarter ever reported to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:59.760
With these types of losses, even a $182&nbsp;
billion bailout would only last a few quarters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:04.800
and that’s why they got such a massive bailout.&nbsp;
The bailout didn’t come for free though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.280
The US government got a 79.9% stake in the company&nbsp;
for the first $85 billion. And AIG had to pay back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:16.400
the remaining bailout with interest. Fortunately,&nbsp;
this was enough money to sustain their losses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:21.520
get them through the recession which is what&nbsp;
AIG really needed. I mean, it’s not like AIG&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:27.440
was the next Radioshack or Sears. Their business&nbsp;
model was still profitable. The problem was that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:32.160
it only worked in bull markets, so they just&nbsp;
had to get through the bear market, and that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:37.520
exactly what the government helped them do. As&nbsp;
the economy recovered, so did AIG’s bottom line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:42.560
and they would become profitable again in late&nbsp;
2010. This allowed them to pay off the bailout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:47.920
with interest. At the same time, the treasury&nbsp;
was slowly selling off AIG stock on the market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:53.680
And by the time everything was said and done,&nbsp;
the US actually ended up profiting $22.7 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.640 --> 00:11:58.160
Meanwhile, things have worked&nbsp;
out pretty well for AIG as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.400
They’ve downsized significantly since their&nbsp;
peak, but they’re still worth over $40 billion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.080
so they’re still pretty big. Considering this&nbsp;
happy ending, some have argued that bailouts don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:14.000
have to be bad if done correctly. But in the end,&nbsp;
I think it just comes down to a consent issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.400
With such bailouts, the government is essentially&nbsp;
making everyday Americans a shareholder in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.400 --> 00:12:22.880
garbage companies which is something that&nbsp;
taxpayers never consented to. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:28.240
bailouts can work out like with AIG, but by&nbsp;
definition, bailouts are handed out to companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:34.880
with the most liquidity risk. So, more times than&nbsp;
not, it’s not gonna work out. But even if it did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:39.680
I’m still not a fan of bailouts given that they’re&nbsp;
not approved by the people that are funding it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:45.840
Also, I think it’s probably best if such companies&nbsp;
go under anyway. But that’s just what I think. Do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:50.960
you think AIG should’ve been bailed out? Comment&nbsp;
that down below. Also, drop a like if you’re not a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:55.120
fan of bailouts either. And of course, consider&nbsp;
checking out our discord community to suggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:58.720
future video ideas and consider subscribing&nbsp;
